## Introduction

This project explores the relationship between the audio characteristics of songs and their popularity on Spotify. Using the Spotify Web API, I collect data on various features such as danceability, energy, valence, and tempo to answer the following question: **What audio features are most predictive of a song's popularity?**

Understanding these relationships can provide insights into trends in music preferences and help artists or producers tailor songs for broader reach.

## Methods

The final dataset being used is built by merging two datasets together:

- An open-source dataset of Spotify track audio features collected from Kaggle (Spotify Tracks Dataset - Maharshi Pandya)
- Real-time Spotify API data, used to retrieve updated popularity scores for each track


### Data Collection
The Kaggle dataset SpotifyFeatures.csv provides:
- Audio features such as danceability, energy, valence, loudness, tempo, and duration_ms
- Metadata including track_id, track_name, artist_name, and genre

The Spotify API was used to fetch current popularity for a sample of tracks.

```{r, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results="hide"}
spotify_raw <- read_csv("SpotifyFeatures.csv")

set.seed(2025)  # for reproducibility
spotify_df <- spotify_raw %>%
  select(track_id, track_name, artist_name, genre,
         danceability, energy, valence, acousticness,
         instrumentalness, liveness, loudness, speechiness,
         tempo, duration_ms) %>%
  distinct(track_id, .keep_all = TRUE) %>%
  sample_n(300) 

dim(spotify_df)
head(spotify_df)
```



```{r, message=FALSE, warning=FALSE, results="hide"}
